== Formal-language / automata audit ==

[[Formal language]] is defined as a set of strings, not the grammar or recognizer that specifies it.

[[Automaton (computation)]] is model-plural across finite automata, pushdown automata and Turing-like machines.

[[State machine]] remains the broader state/transition modeling abstraction used across software, hardware, protocols and control.

Finite-state machine is not treated as the definition of all state machines.

== Turing-machine / computability audit ==

[[Turing machine]] is an abstract mathematical model, not modern hardware.

The [[Church–Turing thesis]] is referenced as a thesis relating informal effective calculability to formal models, while equivalence results among formal models are mathematical theorems.

[[Computability]] separates:

* computable from undecidable;
* decidable from recognizable;
* existence of an algorithm from its resource cost.

== Turing-completeness audit ==

[[Turing completeness]] is explicitly scoped to computational-system universality.

The pages preserve:

<pre>
Turing complete ≠ fast
Turing complete ≠ practical
Turing complete ≠ all program properties decidable
</pre>

Idealized unbounded resource assumptions are explicit.

Specialized recursion-theory use of “Turing complete” for sets/degrees is acknowledged but not conflated with the programming-language/system sense.

== Complexity audit ==

[[Computational complexity]] separates problem/resource classification from one implementation's observed runtime.

Worst-case, average-case and other analysis regimes are distinguished.

Complexity follows computability conceptually:

<pre>
computable? → what resources are required?
</pre>

but practical performance also depends on constants, hardware, locality and workload.

== Architecture / operating-system audit ==

[[Computer architecture]] separates ISA-level architectural behavior from microarchitecture.

[[Operating system]] separates program from process and OS from kernel.

The architecture/OS relationship is represented as mechanism plus resource-management/abstraction rather than as interchangeable layers.

== Network / distributed-system audit ==

[[Computer network]] provides communication substrate and protocol structure.

[[Distributed system]] adds coordinated computation, application state, partial failure and distributed guarantees.

The page also separates distributed-computing notions of consistency from [[Consistency (logic)|logical consistency]].

No connection is made to [[Distributed cognition]] beyond an explicit non-equivalence.

== Database audit ==

[[Database]] explicitly separates the persistent organized data from DBMS software.

Relational databases remain one data model among several.

The database Technical page distinguishes ACID “consistency” from logical consistency.

== Compiler audit ==

[[Compiler]] is defined by translation rather than optimization.

Compiler and interpreter are kept distinct but combinable.

The Technical surface separates parsing, semantic analysis, intermediate representation, optimization, code generation and semantic-preservation concerns.

== Security / cryptography audit ==

[[Computer security]] is threat-model-relative and system-level.

It is not reduced to the CIA triad, though confidentiality/integrity/availability remain central reference properties.

Authentication and authorization remain distinct.

[[Cryptography]] is a mechanism/protocol field inside a broader security system.

It is not reduced to encryption; hashing, signatures, MACs, key exchange, commitments, secure computation and zero-knowledge are represented as distinct mechanism families.

Strong primitives are not claimed to guarantee secure composition or implementation.

== HCI audit ==

[[Human-computer interaction]] is broader than graphical user-interface design.

The wave keeps usability, accessibility, cognitive factors, evaluation, social context and automation within HCI scope.

HCI uses [[Cognition]], [[Attention]], [[Memory]] and [[Cognitive load]] without redefining them.

== Esoteric-programming-language audit ==

[[Esoteric programming language]] survives the public-usefulness audit.

Its scope is supported by academic work on language design, pedagogy, computation and programming culture rather than only community novelty.

The page explicitly preserves:

<pre>
esolang ≠ useless language
esolang ≠ necessarily Turing complete
</pre>

No specific project DSL is used to define the category.

